        <div id="isolate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is 100% Whey Isolate Better Than Grass-Fed Whey Concentrate?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Isolate is better for anyone limiting carbs, fat or lactose, and grass-fed concentrate is better for anyone who values sourcing and a less processed protein over the last few grams of macros. That is the real choice behind Isopure vs Levels. Isopure lists whey protein isolate as its only protein, while Levels lists only whey protein concentrate, which it describes as made from grass-fed, hormone-free dairy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Isolate is concentrate that has been filtered further, removing most of the fat and milk sugar. You can see the result on these two panels: Isopure Zero Carb has 0.5 g of fat and no carbohydrate, while Levels has 2.5 g of fat and 3 g of carbohydrate. Scaled to the same 25 g of protein, Levels comes to about 135 calories and Isopure stays at 110, a gap of roughly 25 calories per shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lactose is where the difference matters most for some people. Lactose is a sugar, so it is counted in the sugar line. Levels says its whey has no added sugar, which suggests most of its 2 g of sugar is naturally occurring lactose from the concentrate. Isopure lists 0 g of sugar, which under US rounding rules means less than 0.5 g per scoop. Neither brand publishes a lactose figure, so this is an inference from the labels, not a measurement.
          </p>

          <p>
            For keto, the arithmetic is simple. Two scoops of Isopure Zero Carb add nothing to a 20 to 30 g daily carb limit; two scoops of Levels add about 6 g. If you are lactose sensitive, 2 g of mostly lactose is less than a splash of milk and many people tolerate it, but people who react to small amounts, or who take several scoops a day, are better served by the isolate. Our <a href='/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/'>lactose-free protein powder</a> guide covers options for stricter needs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does Levels make a whey isolate?</h3>

          <p>
            Not that we could find. The Levels range we track is a grass-fed whey concentrate and a micellar casein, and our <a href='/levels-protein-powder-review/'>Levels review</a> found no Levels isolate on Amazon, where the brand mainly sells. So a shopper who wants a grass-fed isolate cannot get one from either brand here: Isopure does not claim grass-fed sourcing, only rBST-free whey, and Levels does not make an isolate. Our <a href='/best-grass-fed-whey-protein/'>best grass-fed whey protein</a> guide lists brands that offer both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Which Has More Protein per Scoop, Isopure or Levels?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Isopure has slightly more protein per scoop and noticeably more per gram of powder. Per the labels, Isopure Zero Carb gives 25 g in a 31 g scoop, about 81% protein by weight, and Levels gives 24 g in a 32 g scoop, 75%. Isopure Low Carb also gives 25 g, but in a 33 g scoop, so its density falls to about 76% once the cocoa is added.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein by weight is the figure to compare, because scoop sizes differ. In Levels, about 8 g of every scoop is something other than protein: mostly the fat, lactose and minerals that concentrate keeps, plus vanilla extract, lecithin, salt and the sweeteners. In Isopure Zero Carb, about 6 g is not protein, and much of that is the vitamin and mineral blend, lecithin, salt and xanthan gum rather than milk fat or sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a 30 g post-workout serving, you would need about 1.2 scoops of Isopure Zero Carb, roughly 132 calories, or 1.25 scoops of Levels, roughly 163 calories. The 1 g gap per scoop is trivial; the 30-calorie gap at that serving size matters only if you track calories tightly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Levels does publish its amino acid profile: 2.54 g of leucine per scoop, the amino acid most associated with triggering muscle protein synthesis. Isopure does not publish one, so we cannot compare the two on leucine directly. Both are complete dairy proteins, and at 24 to 25 g per serving either one delivers a full muscle-building dose for most adults.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Isopure vs Levels Protein Powder: Which Is Cheaper per Gram?
          </h2>

          <p>
