        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is Isopure or Levels Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Neither has a sport certification that we could verify. A search of the <a href='https://www.nsfsport.com/' rel='nofollow noopener' target='_blank'>NSF Certified for Sport database</a> for Isopure returned no results on 20 November 2026, and we found no Levels listing with NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice either. Neither Vitacost listing claims a banned-substance certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            Each brand has a partial claim worth knowing about. Our <a href='/isopure-protein-powder-review/'>Isopure review</a> found an overseas retailer describing one Zero Carb flavor as Informed Choice certified, which we could not confirm with the certifier. The Levels listing mentions a Clean Label Project Purity Award, which relates to screening for contaminants such as heavy metals rather than banned substances, and we could not verify a product-specific certificate. We count neither.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most buyers this is a draw, not a dealbreaker. A missing certificate does not mean a powder is contaminated; it means there is no independent record you can look up. For drug-tested athletes it rules both out. A certified isolate solves the problem, and our <a href='/best-whey-protein-isolate/'>isolate guide</a> flags which ones carry a certificate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our database also holds no published heavy-metal results for either powder, so we cannot compare them on lead or cadmium. If contaminant testing is your priority, ask each brand for a recent certificate of analysis before you buy, and treat an award logo as a prompt to check rather than proof.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Has the Cleaner Label, Isopure or Levels?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Levels has the cleaner label by most definitions: six ingredients, plant-based sweeteners, no artificial flavors and no soy. Per the Vitacost listing, Levels Vanilla Bean is whey protein concentrate, vanilla extract, sunflower lecithin, sea salt, stevia leaf extract and monk fruit extract. Isopure Zero Carb Creamy Vanilla lists whey protein isolate, a blend of about 20 vitamin and mineral compounds, soy lecithin, natural and artificial flavor, salt, sucralose and xanthan gum.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sweetener is the biggest practical difference. Sucralose is an artificial sweetener approved by the FDA and used in small amounts; stevia and monk fruit are plant-derived and are what many buyers mean by a clean label. Neither choice is a safety issue at these levels, but if you have already decided to avoid sucralose, Levels fits and flavored Isopure does not. Our Isopure review notes that Isopure's Unflavored Zero Carb drops the sweetener and flavorings altogether, which is the Isopure to choose in that case.
          </p>

          <p>
            The vitamin blend is a mixed blessing. It adds micronutrients some buyers like, but if you already take a multivitamin you are doubling up, and it lengthens the label. Isopure's 190 mg of sodium per scoop, against Levels' 125 mg, comes partly from added salt. Xanthan gum is a common thickener that our scoring counts as an additive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allergens and sourcing also lean towards Levels. Isopure uses soy lecithin and lists soy as an allergen; Levels uses sunflower lecithin and says it is soy-free. Levels claims grass-fed, hormone-free dairy, while Isopure claims rBST-free whey, meaning no synthetic growth hormone, but makes no grass-fed claim. Both are labelled gluten-free. Where Isopure's label is cleaner is the nutrition panel itself: less fat, no sugar.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Tastes Better and Mixes Easier?
          </h2>

          <p>
            We have not taste-tested either powder, so we cannot name a winner; what we can say is that Levels offers more flavors and its label points to a richer shake. Vitacost lists nine Levels flavors, including Vanilla Bean, Double Chocolate, Chocolate Peanut Butter, Cappuccino, Vanilla Cinnamon and Unflavored. It lists five Isopure Zero Carb flavors, such as Creamy Vanilla, Banana Cream and Cookies and Cream, and six Low Carb flavors, which is where Isopure keeps its chocolates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Texture follows the macros. Levels' 2.5 g of fat and the extra milk solids in concentrate usually make a creamier shake, and sunflower lecithin helps it disperse. Isopure Zero Carb has almost no fat, which tends to make an isolate thinner and more watery, and Isopure adds xanthan gum to give it more body. Both use lecithin and are sold as shaker-bottle powders that do not need a blender.
          </p>

          <p>
            On buyer feedback, our Levels review found that Amazon buyers rate Levels highly for taste. The other taste difference is the sweetener: stevia and monk fruit can leave a faint aftertaste some people notice, while sucralose has a sweeter, more familiar profile. If you already know which of these you dislike, that is a better guide than any rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            For mixing into food, both brands sell an unflavored version, which is the most flexible choice for oats, yogurt and baking. If you are new to either brand, the safest route is a smaller tub first, even though it costs more per gram, before committing to 3 lb of Isopure or 5 lb of Levels.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pros-cons" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. What Isopure and Levels Each Do Well and Where They Fall Short
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Isopure Zero Carb</h3>

          <p>
            Isopure's strength is the panel: 25 g of protein, 0 g of carbohydrate, 0 g of sugar and 0.5 g of fat for 110 calories, from a single protein source. At about 81% protein by weight it is the densest powder of the three here, which is why it posts the top Label Score, 63 out of 100. For keto dieters and lactose-sensitive stomachs, few mainstream tubs match it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Its weak points are the label and the price. Sucralose, artificial flavor, soy lecithin, xanthan gum, salt and a long vitamin blend make it the opposite of a short-label powder, and 190 mg of sodium is high for a scoop of protein. At about $2.04 per 25 g it is mid-priced for an isolate, and we found no certification. Our <a href='/isopure-protein-powder-review/'>full Isopure review</a> covers Unflavored, Clear and the rest of the range.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Levels Grass-Fed Whey</h3>

          <p>
            Levels does sourcing and simplicity well: grass-fed, hormone-free whey, six ingredients, stevia and monk fruit, no soy, and a published amino profile with 2.54 g of leucine. In the 5 lb tub it is the cheaper of the two at about $1.91 per 25 g, and nine flavors give it more range than Isopure Zero Carb.
          </p>

          <p>
            It falls short on the things concentrate always gives up: 3 g of carbohydrate, 2 g of sugar, 2.5 g of fat and 130 calories, at 75% protein by weight. It has no certification we could find, its own website was not working when we checked, and the 2 lb tub loses the price advantage. Its Label Score is 59 out of 100. Our <a href='/levels-protein-powder-review/'>Levels protein powder review</a> also covers its micellar casein.
          </p>
        </div>

