            13. Who Should Skip Both?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip both if you are a drug-tested athlete. Neither Isopure nor Levels had a sport certification we could verify, and in tested sport the certificate is the point. A whey isolate listed with NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport gives you the low-lactose label of Isopure and a record you can check.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip both, too, if you have a milk allergy or eat vegan. Both are dairy proteins, and isolate removes lactose, not milk protein, so Isopure is no safer for an allergy than Levels. A pea, soy or blended plant protein is the right category.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, skip both if price per gram is all that matters. At about $1.91 to $2.04 per 25 g they sit in the middle of the market, and store-brand or bulk unflavored wheys often cost less. Our <a href='/protein-powder-comparison/'>protein powder comparison</a> lets you sort every product we have checked by cost per 25 g.
