        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            15. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming grass-fed means isolate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Levels is grass-fed whey concentrate, not isolate, and grass-fed describes the cows, not the filtering. It still carries 3 g of carbohydrate and 2 g of sugar per scoop. If you want low lactose and grass-fed sourcing together, neither brand here offers both; look for a grass-fed isolate from another company instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the 2 lb Levels tub to save money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Levels beats Isopure on price only in the 5 lb tub, at about $1.91 per 25 g. The 2 lb tub on Vitacost came to about $2.16 per 25 g, more than Isopure Zero Carb's 3 lb tub. Work out cost per 25 g for the size you will actually buy before treating either brand as the cheap one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Picking Isopure Low Carb for a cheaper chocolate shake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Low Carb is the most expensive powder in this comparison at about $2.39 per 25 g, and it adds 20 mg more sodium than Zero Carb. If you want chocolate and can live with 3 g of carbs, Levels sells Double Chocolate and its 5 lb tub cost less per gram at the price we recorded; if you need an isolate, Zero Carb plus cocoa powder is cheaper.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading an award logo as a sport certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Levels' listing mentions a Clean Label Project Purity Award, and one overseas retailer has called an Isopure flavor Informed Choice certified. We could not verify either. Contaminant awards are not banned-substance testing, so drug-tested athletes should look up the exact product in the NSF or Informed Sport database first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overlooking soy and sweetener lines on the label</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flavored Isopure uses soy lecithin and sucralose; Levels uses sunflower lecithin with stevia and monk fruit. For someone with a soy allergy or a firm no-sucralose rule, those two lines decide the comparison before macros or price do. Isopure Unflavored removes the sweetener but check its lecithin source on the tub.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            16. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Isopure better than Levels protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It depends on your priority. Isopure Zero Carb is better for macros, with 25 g of protein, 0 g of carbohydrate and 110 calories per scoop, and a higher Label Score (63 vs 59). Levels is better for ingredients: grass-fed whey, stevia and monk fruit, and no soy. Levels' 5 lb tub is also slightly cheaper, about $1.91 per 25 g against $2.04.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Levels whey isolate or concentrate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Levels Grass-Fed Whey is a whey protein concentrate, per its ingredient list, made from grass-fed, hormone-free dairy according to the brand. That is why it carries 3 g of carbohydrate, 2 g of sugar and 2.5 g of fat per scoop. We did not find a Levels whey isolate; the brand's other powder that we track is a micellar casein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which is better for lactose intolerance, Isopure or Levels?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Isopure Zero Carb, because it is pure whey isolate and lists 0 g of sugar, meaning under 0.5 g per scoop. Levels lists 2 g of sugar with no added sugar, so most of that is likely lactose. Neither publishes a lactose figure or claims to be lactose-free, and neither suits a milk allergy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Isopure or Levels third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Neither has a sport certification we could verify. NSF Certified for Sport returned no Isopure results on 20 November 2026, and we found no Levels listing with NSF, Informed Sport or Informed Choice. Levels mentions a Clean Label Project award, which we could not confirm and which is not banned-substance testing. Drug-tested athletes should choose a certified whey.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which is better for keto, Isopure or Levels?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Isopure Zero Carb. It lists 0 g of carbohydrate per scoop, so it adds nothing to a daily carb limit, while Levels lists 3 g per scoop, about 6 g for two shakes. Levels does have more fat, 2.5 g against 0.5 g, but not enough to matter for most keto plans. Isopure Low Carb sits between them at 1 g.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Isopure or Levels have artificial sweeteners?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Flavored Isopure Zero Carb and Low Carb use sucralose, an artificial sweetener, plus natural and artificial flavor, per the labels we read. Levels uses stevia leaf extract and monk fruit extract and states no artificial flavors or sweeteners. If you want an Isopure with no sweetener, the Unflavored Zero Carb leaves it out.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Why does Isopure Zero Carb have more sodium than Levels?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Isopure lists added salt and a vitamin and mineral blend, and the label shows 190 mg of sodium per scoop against Levels' 125 mg. Low Carb Dutch Chocolate reaches 210 mg. Spread across a day these are modest amounts, but if you track sodium, Levels is the lower option of the two brands.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

